```javascript
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, budget) {
    let closestSum = -1; // Initialize with -1 to handle cases where no valid sum is found

    // Iterate over each element in the first array
    for (let i = 0; i < arr1.length; i++) {
        // Iterate over each element in the second array
        for (let j = 0; j < arr2.length; j++) {
            const sum = arr1[i] + arr2[j]; // Calculate the sum of the current pair

            // Check if the sum is less than or equal to the budget and closer to the budget than the current closest sum
            if (sum <= budget && sum > closestSum) {
                closestSum = sum; // Update the closest sum
            }
        }
    }

    return closestSum; // Return the closest sum found
}

// Example usage:
const arr1 = [1, 3, 5];
const arr2 = [2, 8, 10];
const budget = 10;
console.log(getExpense(arr1, arr2, budget)); // Output: 10
```

### Explanation:
- We initialize `closestSum` to `-1` to handle cases where no valid sum is found that is less than or equal to the budget.
- We use two nested loops to iterate over each possible pair of elements from `arr1` and `arr2`.
- For each pair, we calculate their sum.
- If the sum is less than or equal to the budget and greater than the current `closestSum`, we update `closestSum`.
- Finally, we return the `closestSum`, which will be the largest sum that is less than or equal to the budget. If no such sum is found, it will return `-1`.

In your scenario, you have multiple types of accounts (e.g., `DoctorAccount`, `UserAccount`) and you anticipate adding more types in the future. A good design pattern to use in this case is the **Factory Method Pattern**. This pattern allows you to create objects without specifying the exact class of object that will be created. It is particularly useful when you have a common interface or superclass for different types of objects.

Here's how you can implement the Factory Method Pattern in your case:

1. **Define a Common Interface or Abstract Class**: Create an interface or abstract class that all account types will implement or extend.

```java
public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
    String getPhone();
    String getFullName();
    // Add other common methods if needed
}
```

2. **Implement the Interface in Your Entities**: Make your entities implement this interface.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_account")
public class DoctorAccount implements Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    private Integer specialtyId;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // Implement interface methods
    @Override
    public Integer getId() {
        return id;
    }

    @Override
    public String getPhone() {
        return phone;
    }

    @Override
    public String getFullName() {
        return fullName;
    }
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "user_account")
public class UserAccount implements Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // Implement interface methods
    @Override
    public Integer getId() {
        return id;
    }

    @Override
    public String getPhone() {
        return phone;
    }

    @Override
    public String getFullName() {
        return fullName;
    }
}
```

3. **Create a Factory Class**: Implement a factory class that will return the appropriate account type based on some criteria.

```java
public class AccountFactory {

    @Autowired
    private DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository;

    @Autowired
    private UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository;

    public Account getAccountById(Integer id, AccountType accountType) {
        switch (accountType) {
            case DOCTOR:
                return doctorAccountRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
            case USER:
                return userAccountRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
            // Add more cases for new account types
            default:
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid account type");
        }
    }
}

public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR,
    USER
    // Add more account types as needed
}
```

4. **Use the Factory in Your Service Layer**: Use the factory to get the appropriate account type in your service layer.

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {

    @Autowired
    private AccountFactory accountFactory;

    public Account getAccountById(Integer id, AccountType accountType) {
        return accountFactory.getAccountById(id, accountType);
    }
}
