In Go, appending to a slice is not an atomic operation. This means that if multiple goroutines try to append to the same slice concurrently without proper synchronization, it can lead to a race condition, resulting in unpredictable behavior or a program crash.

To demonstrate this, you can write a program that spawns multiple goroutines, each attempting to append to a shared slice without synchronization. This will likely cause a race condition, which can be detected using Go's race detector.

Here's a simple program to illustrate this:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	slice := []int{}

	// Number of goroutines
	numGoroutines := 100

	// Add the number of goroutines to the wait group
	wg.Add(numGoroutines)

	for i := 0; i < numGoroutines; i++ {
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			// Append to the slice
			slice = append(slice, i)
		}(i)
	}

	// Wait for all goroutines to finish
	wg.Wait()

	// Print the length of the slice
	fmt.Println("Length of slice:", len(slice))
}
```

To run this program with the race detector, use the following command:

```sh
go run -race main.go
```

The `-race` flag enables the race detector, which will report any race conditions it finds. When you run the program, you should see race condition warnings, indicating that the slice append operation is not atomic and is being accessed concurrently by multiple goroutines.

To fix this issue, you can use a mutex to synchronize access to the slice:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	var mu sync.Mutex
	slice := []int{}

	// Number of goroutines
	numGoroutines := 100

	// Add the number of goroutines to the wait group
	wg.Add(numGoroutines)

	for i := 0; i < numGoroutines; i++ {
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			// Lock the mutex before appending to the slice
			mu.Lock()
			slice = append(slice, i)
			// Unlock the mutex after appending
			mu.Unlock()
		}(i)
	}

	// Wait for all goroutines to finish
	wg.Wait()

	// Print the length of the slice
	fmt.Println("Length of slice:", len(slice))
}
```

By using a mutex, you ensure that only one goroutine can append to the slice at a time, preventing race conditions.

